## Credentials & Authentication

Your credentials have been securely extracted and saved for your own records inside:
`credentials/config.env`

The script does **NOT** read from this file natively. Instead, it securely leverages the local authentication caches from the official `gh` (GitHub) and `glab` (GitLab) CLIs.

### What's happening behind the scenes:
- **GitHub:** Uses the `pheonix14` profile managed by `gh`. (The script actively suppresses any global `GITHUB_TOKEN` environment variables to ensure `gh` uses the exact cached `pheonix14` token).
- **GitLab:** Uses the `thefeonix14` profile managed by `glab` (Securely stored in your Windows Operating System Keyring).

---

## How to Run Everything

### 1. Prerequisites
Ensure you have the following installed on your machine:
- [Git](https://git-scm.com/)
- [Python 3.10+](https://www.python.org/)
- [GitHub CLI (gh)](https://cli.github.com/)
- [GitLab CLI (glab)](https://gitlab.com/gitlab-org/cli)

### 2. Install Python Dependencies
The script relies on the `rich` library for the terminal UI. Install it via pip:
```powershell
pip install rich
```

### 3. Run the Tool (`main.py`)
Simply execute `main.py` for an interactive menu or CLI flags:

```powershell
# Interactive Menu Mode
python main.py

# Run Full Backup
python main.py --backup

# Target Specific Repositories
python main.py -r repo1 repo2

# Run Backup Verification & Auto-Sync Missing Repos
python main.py --verify --yes

# Quick Environment Status Check
python main.py --status
```

### 4. What to Expect
- The script will query GitHub for all your repositories (currently 140+).
- It will loop through each one, verifying if a private mirror exists on GitLab (and creating it if not).
- A local mirrored copy of the repository will be placed in the `/repos/` folder.
- It will push a perfect mirror to your GitLab account.
- **If any repository crashes** (like a random Windows file lock), the script will skip it and proceed, meaning you can just re-run the script later to catch any ones that failed!

---

## Troubleshooting

- **`Permission denied` or `invalid index-pack output` on a repo:** 
  If you see an error about `unable to unlink` or `unable to write file`, this is a standard Windows file-locking issue (an antivirus or a hidden background `git.exe` is locking the file). **Solution:** The new script automatically skips these! If you want to force it to retry, open Task Manager, end any lingering `git.exe` tasks, delete the corrupted folder in `repos/`, and re-run `python backup_script.py`.
